  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/3/35/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_56.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Giant-Man enters his lab in the upper floors of a building and decides it's time to offer Jan \"an engagement ring and ask her to marry me!\"&nbsp; But when Jan arrives, she despairs of him ever proposing marriage and decides to make him jealous by announcing that she's off to a party, leaving Hank distraught, wondering what a fool he's been.&nbsp; She's great as his partner, \"but when it comes to <i>romance</i>, she wants someone as wealthy as she!\"&nbsp; Ah well, perhaps it's all for the best!\n\nBut an observer ant notifies Hank that a strange, suspicious-looking stage magician is planning to perform where Sterling Stuyvesant is hosting the party - the very same one which Jan is attending!&nbsp; Oh no!&nbsp; There's not one moment to lose!\n\nAt the party, the magician is introduced and begins to entertain the crowd as expected.&nbsp; But soon his real motives are made clear, and Jan shrinks down to confront him as the Wasp.&nbsp; But he uses his suction wand to pull her inside and trap her!&nbsp; After robbing the guests, he makes his way out!&nbsp; Hank arrives too late and must return to his lab to try and find Jan.&nbsp; But he soon finds that even his ants can't locate him.\n\nHe must lure the Magician into a trap, so he stages a society party abord a yacht named \"Neptune.\"&nbsp; But Hank waits alone on the yacht, waiting for the Magician to take the bait.&nbsp; He stages it to look like there's a major party going on, and sure enough the Magician arrives, only to find Giant-Man waiting for him.&nbsp; He immediately retreats to his blimp, which is painted blue to blend in with the sky while Giant-Man is temporarily distracted saving a capsizing boat.\n\nBut as the blimps heads off, Hank snatches hold of it with a rope and shrinks so he can climb aboard.&nbsp; He frees the Wasp, due to his keeping his normal strength even while reduced in size.&nbsp; (<i>Apparently the same is not true for the Wasp</i>?)&nbsp; Wasp then goes to the blimp's release valve and deflates the huge ship as the now-enlarged Giant-Man confronts the Magician.\n\nThe blimp goes down, and Jan temporarily thinks she's lost Hank forever, but of course not - Hank appears, and she declares \"there can <i>never be anyone else for me!</i>\"&nbsp; To which Hank replies \"There never <i>will</i> be, Jan.&nbsp; I promise you that!\"\n\nThe day is saved...",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "argh, this was about as barf-inducing a plot as ever for this magazine.&nbsp; The villain was so lame, I didn't even care as I trudged through the pages and found I simply could not wait for the story to be over.&nbsp; The drama of the romance at the beginning might have been a step forward in their relationship, but naturally it was turned on its ear with Jan hoping to foster jealousy, leading Hank to feel like he wasn't worthy of her.&nbsp; And even though they seem to realize at the end how each feels for the other, I'd bet anything that we won't be seeing that engagement ring again!&nbsp; (Obviously since we know they don't actually get married until around Avengers #60 or thereabouts, years later.)\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nI struggle to understand how this magazine stayed in publication.&nbsp; Giant-Man and the Wasp are part of the Avengers, sure, so that was bound to help some, but their solo adventures so far have been about as bad as they come, and I find myself dreading the days when I'm forced to wade through another issue of Tales to Astonish (which more accurately should be named Tales to Abolish!)\n\nSide Note:&nbsp; I can't help but wonder why it is that normal strength was clearly enough to free the Wasp from her small glass prison, but that she didn't even seem to try, unless it's the case that she doesn't share this same ability that Hank has when he's ant-sized.&nbsp; I can think of no other explanation as to why she didn't do exactly what Hank did and simply tear open her prison.",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>The Wasp Tells a Tale:</b>  \"Beware the Bog Beast!\"\n(by Stan Lee, Larry Lieber and Paul Reinman)\n\nThe Wasp distracts Giant-Man during a workout by posing a riddle.\n\nOn a far-off world, a princess falls in love with a poor peasant (Lorenzo), and her brother, the ruler, swears to get rid of the interloper by coming up with a contest:&nbsp; only the one who can vanquish the Bog Beast shall have his sister's hand in marriage!\n\nOnly three men are brave enough to even try, including Lorenzo, who is content to allow the other two stronger men go first.&nbsp; But neither the strong man or the warrior has any luck.\n\nBut the Wasp refuses to tell Giant-Man how Lorenzo won the right to marry the princess -- unless Hank takes her out to dinner!&nbsp; It's blackmail, but Hank agrees.&nbsp; Only then does she explain that \"lazy\" Lorenzo did nothing at all -- he simply told the princess to cross a bridge that the bog beast was too heavy to follow across.&nbsp; Mission accomplished!\n\nHank is disgusted -- he'd rather face a super-villain than another of Jan's riddles!"
  ],
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}